# Dino Detectives

Description => Five rounds of dinosaurs, fossils and very big teeth. Made for curious kids and the grown-ups beside them.
Author => Pandaqi
Language => en
Seed => dino-detectives
Syntax => 3
Theme => jurassic
BannerImage => media/banner.jpg
Celebration => true
Streaks => true
Lifelines => 50/50 | extra-time

Type => Start
Question => Dino Detectives
Comment => Five rounds. One point a question unless it says otherwise. Bring a pencil for the picture round, or use the buttons.
Buttons => play | interactive | answers | settings

## Meet the dinosaurs

Question => What does the word "dinosaur" mean?
Answers => Terrible lizard
Type => Open
AlsoAccept => Fearful lizard | Terrible reptile | Terrible lizards
Grading => normalized
Hints => The first half means "fearful" or "terrible". The second half means "lizard".
Explanation => From the Greek deinos (terrible) and sauros (lizard). Richard Owen made up the name in 1842.
Category => Words

Question => Which of these was NOT a dinosaur?
Answers => T. rex | Triceratops | Pterodactyl | Stegosaurus
Type => Choice
Correct => Pterodactyl
Feedback => A dinosaur, and a famous one. | A dinosaur with a frill. | A pterosaur. It flew, but it was not a dinosaur. | A dinosaur with plates on its back.
Explanation => Pterodactyls were pterosaurs: flying cousins of the dinosaurs, not dinosaurs themselves.
Category => Families

Question => Name the three periods of the Mesozoic Era. Any order.
#### Answers
Triassic
Jurassic
Cretaceous
Type => List
Scoring => partial
Grading => normalized
Suggest => Triassic | Jurassic | Cretaceous
Score => 3
Explanation => Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous. The dinosaurs lived through all three, for about 165 million years.
Category => Timeline

Question => True or false about dinosaurs?
Answers => True | False
Rows => Some dinosaurs had feathers | Every dinosaur was huge | Birds are living dinosaurs | Every dinosaur ate meat
Correct => True | False | True | False
Type => Matrix
Scoring => partial
Score => 4
Explanation => Feathers, yes. Huge, no: many were the size of a chicken. Birds are the dinosaurs that survived. And plenty of dinosaurs ate plants.
Category => Facts

Question => A dinosaur that eats only plants is called a [blank].
Answers => herbivore
Type => Cloze
AlsoAccept => plant-eater | plant eater | a herbivore
Grading => normalized
Hints => It starts with "herb".
Explanation => Herbivore. A meat-eater is a carnivore. An omnivore eats both.
Category => Words

## Bones and fossils

Question => Put each label on the right spot in the dig site.
Description => A fossil dig with a dinosaur skull, a leg bone and an egg.
Media => media/dig-site.svg { alt="A cartoon fossil dig with a dinosaur skull, a long leg bone and an egg", credit="Made for this quiz" }
Type => Diagram
Answers => Skull | Leg bone | Egg
Targets => A @ 20,30 | B @ 50,72 | C @ 79,32
Correct => Skull @ A | Leg bone @ B | Egg @ C
Scoring => partial
Score => 3
Hints => The skull is the round thing with teeth. The egg is the oval on the right.
Explanation => Three finds, one hole in the ground. A real dig is slower and involves more tea.
Category => Digging

Question => Click the amber with the insect inside.
Media => media/amber.svg { alt="Three pieces of amber on a wooden table, one with a small insect trapped inside", credit="Made for this quiz" }
Answers => 69,48
Type => Hotspot
HitRadius => 7
Score => 2
Hints => Look for the piece with legs and antennae.
Explanation => Amber is tree resin that went hard over millions of years. Small creatures sometimes got stuck in it, which is very bad luck for the insect and very good luck for us.
Category => Digging

Question => About how many million years ago did the non-bird dinosaurs disappear?
Answers => 66
Type => Range
Range => 5
Unit => million years
Hints => It is between 60 and 70.
Explanation => About 66 million years ago. The last of the non-bird dinosaurs were gone, and the age of mammals began.
Category => Timeline

Question => What is this spiral fossil called?
Media => media/ammonite.jpg { alt="A spiral ammonite fossil in a square block of grey stone", credit="Made for this quiz" }
Answers => Ammonite
Type => Open
AlsoAccept => Ammonites | Ammonoid | Ammonoids
Grading => normalized
Hints => It starts with "amm".
Explanation => An ammonite. These sea creatures lived in coiled shells and died out with the dinosaurs.
Category => Fossils

Question => A scientist who studies fossils is called a [blank].
Answers => palaeontologist
Type => Cloze
AlsoAccept => paleontologist | palaeontologists | paleontologists
Grading => normalized
Suggest => palaeontologist | paleontologist
Hints => The American spelling drops the "a".
Explanation => Palaeontologist. In the US it is spelled paleontologist. Either way, they spend a lot of time with a small brush.
Category => Words

Question => How long could a T. rex grow, from nose to tail?
Answers => 12
Type => Estimate
Tolerance => 2
Unit => metres
Slider => 4..20
Score => 2
Hints => About the length of a bus.
Explanation => Around 12 metres. Its teeth were up to 20 centimetres long, and its arms were famously small.
Category => Giants

## Dinner time

Question => Put each dinosaur in the right lunch box.
Type => Classification
Answers => T. rex | Triceratops | Stegosaurus | Velociraptor | Brachiosaurus
Buckets => Meat | Plants
Correct => T. rex @ Meat | Velociraptor @ Meat | Triceratops @ Plants | Stegosaurus @ Plants | Brachiosaurus @ Plants
Scoring => partial
Score => 5
Explanation => T. rex and Velociraptor hunted. The others ate plants, and spent a lot of the day chewing.
Category => Food

Question => Which one did not eat meat?
Answers => T. rex | Velociraptor | Triceratops | Spinosaurus
Type => OddOneOut
Correct => Triceratops
AlsoAccept => it ate plants | plants | it was a plant eater | because it ate plants
Hints => Look at the horns.
Explanation => Triceratops had a beak for cropping plants. The other three were hunters.
Category => Food

Question => Which of these did NOT live in the Cretaceous period?
Answers => Triceratops | T. rex | Stegosaurus | Ankylosaurus
Type => Choice
Correct => Triceratops | T. rex | Ankylosaurus
Negate => true
Scoring => partial
Feedback => Cretaceous, and very common. | Cretaceous, and very toothy. | Jurassic. Wrong period. | Cretaceous, with a club on its tail.
Score => 3
Explanation => Stegosaurus lived in the Jurassic, tens of millions of years before the others.
Category => Timeline

Question => Match each dinosaur to its nickname.
#### Answers
T. rex ~ Tyrant king
Triceratops ~ Three-horned face
Stegosaurus ~ Roof lizard
Velociraptor ~ Swift thief
Type => Match
Scoring => partial
Score => 4
Reveal => zoom-in
Explanation => The names are descriptions in Greek. Tyrannosaurus rex really does mean "tyrant lizard king".
Category => Words

## Giants and small fries

Question => Rank these dinosaurs from longest to shortest.
Answers => Argentinosaurus | Brachiosaurus | Triceratops | Velociraptor
Type => Rank
Scoring => partial
Correct => Argentinosaurus | Brachiosaurus | Triceratops | Velociraptor
Score => 3
Hints => The chicken-sized one goes last.
Explanation => Argentinosaurus may have reached 30 metres. Brachiosaurus was around 20. Triceratops was about 9, and Velociraptor about 2.
Category => Giants

Question => How many horns did Triceratops have, and how many claws on each T. rex hand?
Type => Parts
Scoring => partial
Parts => number: Horns: 2 | number: Claws: 2
Answers => 3 || 2
Score => 4
Hints => One horn sits on the nose. The other two are above the eyes.
Explanation => Three horns, two claws. The T. rex arms were short, but they were not useless.
Category => Body

Question => Sort these from smallest to biggest.
Answers => Velociraptor | Triceratops | Brachiosaurus | Argentinosaurus
Type => Sort
Scoring => partial
AnswersOrder => shuffle
Score => 4
Explanation => Velociraptor, Triceratops, Brachiosaurus, Argentinosaurus. The last one is only known from a handful of bones.
Category => Giants

Question => Which of these is a Triceratops?
Answers => media/triceratops.svg { alt="A cartoon dinosaur with three horns and a big red frill" } | media/stegosaurus.svg { alt="A cartoon dinosaur with orange plates along its back" } | media/brachiosaurus.svg { alt="A cartoon dinosaur with a very long neck" }
Type => Choice
Correct => media/triceratops.svg
Explanation => The horns and the frill are the giveaway. The plates belong to Stegosaurus, and the long neck to Brachiosaurus.
Category => Pictures

Question => In which period did the {1} live?
Answers => Cretaceous | Jurassic | Triassic
Type => Choice
Correct => Cretaceous
Variants => T. rex | Triceratops | Velociraptor
Score => 2
Explanation => All three lived in the Cretaceous. The Jurassic had Stegosaurus and Brachiosaurus.
Category => Timeline

## The last day

Question => What do most scientists think ended the age of the dinosaurs?
Media => media/asteroid.jpg { alt="A bright asteroid streaks across an orange sunset above a green plain with long-necked dinosaurs", credit="Made for this quiz" }
Answers => A giant asteroid | A worldwide flood | A long cold winter | A volcano in France
Type => Choice
Correct => A giant asteroid
Steps => media | question | answers
ColorScheme => night
Hints => It came from space.
Explanation => A 10-kilometre asteroid hit near Chicxulub, in Mexico, about 66 million years ago. The dust darkened the sky and the food chains fell apart.
Category => The end

Question => Which animals alive today are the direct descendants of dinosaurs?
Answers => Birds
Type => Open
AlsoAccept => birds | avian dinosaurs | birds and their cousins
Reject => lizards | crocodiles | mammals
Grading => normalized
Hints => Look up.
Explanation => Birds. Crocodiles are close cousins, but birds are the dinosaurs that made it through.
Category => The end

Question => True or false about the last day of the dinosaurs?
Answers => True | False
Rows => The asteroid hit near Mexico | Every dinosaur died on the same day | Birds survived
Correct => True | False | True
Type => Matrix
Scoring => partial
Score => 3
Explanation => The crater is at Chicxulub. The dying took a long time, and birds survived. They are still everywhere.
Category => The end

Question => From the first dinosaurs to the last, about how many million years passed?
Answers => 165
Type => Range
Range => 20
Unit => million years
Score => 3
Wager => true
Timer => 1:30
Hints => More than 100. Less than 200.
Notes => Take the bets before the reveal. Nothing focuses a room like a wager.
Explanation => About 165 million years, from the Triassic to the end of the Cretaceous. Humans have been here for a few hundred thousand.
Category => Timeline

Question => Riddle: I had three horns, a big frill, and I ate plants. Who am I?
Answers => Triceratops
Type => Open
AlsoAccept => triceratops | Triceratops horridus
Class => center spotlight
Layout => center
Transition => zoom-in
Score => 2
Hints => You met me in the picture round.
Explanation => Triceratops. The frill may have been for display, defence, or both. Nobody can ask one.
Category => Pictures

Type => End
Question => Case closed, detectives.
Comment => That is the end of the dig. If you missed one, the answers are a click away.
